Use this when the sequence should continue as soon as the driver accepts an item. The invariant is that the sequence cannot change an object after the driver accepts it, and every response keeps the identity of its original request.

**Interview answer.** "One thread accepts items, clones them, copies the routing ID, and puts the snapshot in a bounded mailbox before calling `item_done`. A second thread drives each snapshot and returns a response with `put_response`. The bounded mailbox gives the sequence backpressure, and the sequence must drain responses with `get_response`."

```systemverilog
mailbox #(bus_req) pending = new(8);

task accept_requests();
  bus_req req, snap;
  forever begin
    seq_item_port.get_next_item(req);
    if (!$cast(snap, req.clone()))
      `uvm_fatal("COPY", "Request clone failed")
    snap.set_id_info(req);
    pending.put(snap); // Blocks the sequence when the queue is full
    seq_item_port.item_done();
  end
endtask

task execute_requests();
  bus_req snap;
  bus_rsp rsp;
  forever begin
    pending.get(snap);
    rsp = bus_rsp::type_id::create("rsp");
    rsp.set_id_info(snap);
    drive_to_completion(snap, rsp);
    seq_item_port.put_response(rsp);
  end
endtask

task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
  fork
    accept_requests();
    execute_requests();
  join
endtask
```

**Why clone before `item_done`.** `get(req)` finishes the handshake before it returns, so cloning afterward does not protect you from a sequence that reuses the object. Clone between `get_next_item` and `item_done`. Make sure `do_copy` copies every nested object the driver needs.

**Sequence side.** The sequence must call `get_response()` for every item. Otherwise the response queue fills (default depth 8) and UVM reports an overflow error.

