Use this when you define the timing boundary between DUT, driver, and monitor. The invariant is that everyone drives and samples through declared clocking blocks, and request signals stay stable through stalls.

**Interview answer.** "I use two clocking blocks. The driver drives on the falling edge so signals settle well before the DUT samples. The monitor samples on the rising edge with `#1step`, which reads the values from just before the edge, the same values the DUT and the assertions see. I put protocol assertions in the interface so every test gets them."

The DUT owns PREADY, PRDATA, and PSLVERR. The driver owns the request pins. The top owns clock and reset.

```systemverilog
interface apb_if(input logic pclk, input logic presetn);
  timeunit 1ns; timeprecision 1ps;
  logic psel, penable, pwrite;
  logic [31:0] paddr, pwdata, prdata;
  logic [3:0] pstrb;
  logic [2:0] pprot;
  logic pready, pslverr;

  clocking drv_cb @(negedge pclk);
    default input #1step output #0;
    input presetn;
    output psel, penable, pwrite, paddr, pwdata, pstrb, pprot;
  endclocking
  clocking mon_cb @(posedge pclk);
    default input #1step;
    input presetn, psel, penable, pwrite, paddr, pwdata;
    input pstrb, pprot, pready, prdata, pslverr;
  endclocking

  ap_setup: assert property (@(posedge pclk)
    disable iff (presetn !== 1'b1)
    psel && !penable |=> psel && penable &&
      $stable({paddr,pwrite,pwdata,pstrb,pprot}));
  ap_wait: assert property (@(posedge pclk)
    disable iff (presetn !== 1'b1)
    psel && penable && !pready |=> psel && penable &&
      $stable({paddr,pwrite,pwdata,pstrb,pprot}));
  ap_end: assert property (@(posedge pclk)
    disable iff (presetn !== 1'b1)
    psel && penable && pready |=> !penable);
  ap_read_strobes: assert property (@(posedge pclk)
    disable iff (presetn !== 1'b1)
    psel && !pwrite |-> pstrb == 4'b0);
endinterface
```

**What a full checker adds.** A check that access always follows setup, X checks on control signals, address-range and error expectations, reset properties, and `cover property` statements so you can prove each check fired at least once.

**Counterexample to walk.** PADDR changes during a stalled access, and `ap_wait` fires. A monitor that collects on PREADY alone counts idle cycles as transfers, because PREADY may be high while PSEL is low.

**AHB adaptation (Q177).** AHB is pipelined. The address phase of transfer N+1 overlaps the data phase of transfer N, and HREADY low stretches both. Put HTRANS, HADDR, HWRITE, HSIZE, HBURST, and HWDATA in the driver clocking block, and sample HREADY, HRDATA, and HRESP in the monitor block. Template 10 shows the monitor logic.

