Use this to generate legal scenarios and to coordinate several sequencers. The invariant is that a sequence creates a fresh object for every request, consumes every response, and does not confuse "stimulus finished" with "verification finished".

**Interview answer.** "`start_item` waits for the sequencer to grant arbitration. I randomize between `start_item` and `finish_item` so late randomization can react to current state. `finish_item` returns when the driver calls `item_done`. That tells me the driver finished the handshake, not that the DUT was correct. The scoreboard decides correctness."

In this example the test owns the objection. The sequence only generates requests and reads responses.

```systemverilog
class apb_smoke_seq extends uvm_sequence #(apb_item);
  `uvm_object_utils(apb_smoke_seq)
  function new(string name = "apb_smoke_seq");
    super.new(name);
  endfunction
  task body();
    apb_item q, r;
    for (int pass = 0; pass < 2; pass++) begin
      for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) begin
        q = apb_item::type_id::create("q");
        start_item(q);
        q.addr = 4*i;
        q.write = (pass == 0);
        q.strb = q.write ? 4'hf : 4'h0;
        q.wdata = 32'ha5a50000 ^ i;
        finish_item(q);
        get_response(r);
        if (r.status != APB_OK)
          `uvm_fatal("SMOKE", "Unexpected error/reset in smoke test")
      end
    end
  endtask
endclass
```

**Randomized variant.** Between `start_item(q)` and `finish_item(q)`, call `if (!q.randomize() with {write == 1; strb == 4'hf;}) `uvm_fatal(...)`. Do not consume time in that window. A reset-stress sequence must expect `APB_RESET`, decide whether to retry, and rewrite memory before reading it again.

### Virtual sequence adaptation

A virtual sequence coordinates child sequences on real sequencers. Each child gets its own object and an explicit sequencer handle.

```systemverilog
class dual_apb_vseq extends uvm_sequence;
  `uvm_object_utils(dual_apb_vseq)
  uvm_sequencer #(apb_item) left_sqr, right_sqr;
  function new(string name = "dual_apb_vseq");
    super.new(name);
  endfunction
  task body();
    apb_smoke_seq left, right;
    if (left_sqr == null || right_sqr == null)
      `uvm_fatal("NOSQR", "Child sequencer handle missing")
    left = apb_smoke_seq::type_id::create("left");
    right = apb_smoke_seq::type_id::create("right");
    fork
      left.start(left_sqr);
      right.start(right_sqr);
    join
  endtask
endclass
```

**`start(null)` (Q246).** Assign both handles, then call `vseq.start(null)`. That is legal because the virtual sequence never sends items itself. A sequence that sends items with `start_item` needs a real sequencer, and with `null` it fails at `start_item`.

**Virtual sequencer pattern (Q245).** The alternative stores the child sequencer handles in a virtual sequencer component, assigned in `connect_phase`. The virtual sequence reaches them through `p_sequencer` after `` `uvm_declare_p_sequencer ``.

**Several sequences on one sequencer (Q244, Q257).** The sequencer arbitrates. Use `set_arbitration(UVM_SEQ_ARB_STRICT_FIFO)` with a priority argument to `start()` for priority, or `lock()`/`grab()` for exclusive access. To trace which sequence produced an item, read `item.get_parent_sequence()` or the sequence ID, and route the response with `set_id_info`.

**Several item types (Q207).** Parameterize the sequencer and driver with a common base class and `$cast` to the subtype in the driver.

**Stress cases.** Two concurrent writers to the same address need an ordering-aware model or disjoint address ranges. Never start one sequence object twice at the same time.

