Use this to turn pin activity into checker input. The invariant is one new transaction per completed transfer. A stalled cycle or a request alone produces nothing.

**Interview answer.** "The monitor is passive and independent of the driver. It samples through the monitor clocking block and publishes a transaction only on the cycle where PSEL, PENABLE, and PREADY are all high. It sends reset as an event in the same analysis stream so the scoreboard sees reset in the right order relative to transfers."

```systemverilog
class apb_monitor extends uvm_monitor;
  `uvm_component_utils(apb_monitor)
  virtual apb_if vif;
  uvm_analysis_port #(apb_item) ap;
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
    ap = new("ap", this);
  endfunction
  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);
    if (!uvm_config_db#(virtual apb_if)::get(this, "", "vif", vif))
      `uvm_fatal("NOVIF", "APB interface missing")
  endfunction
  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    apb_item t;
    bit in_reset = 0;
    forever begin
      @(vif.mon_cb);
      if (vif.mon_cb.presetn !== 1'b1) begin
        if (!in_reset) begin
          t = apb_item::type_id::create("reset_event");
          t.status = APB_RESET; ap.write(t);
        end
        in_reset = 1;
        continue;
      end
      in_reset = 0;
      if ($isunknown({vif.mon_cb.psel,vif.mon_cb.penable}))
        `uvm_fatal("XCTRL", "Unknown request control")
      if (vif.mon_cb.psel && vif.mon_cb.penable) begin
        if ($isunknown(vif.mon_cb.pready))
          `uvm_fatal("XREADY", "Unknown access completion")
        if (vif.mon_cb.pready) begin
          if ($isunknown({vif.mon_cb.paddr,vif.mon_cb.pwrite,
                          vif.mon_cb.pstrb,vif.mon_cb.pslverr}))
            `uvm_fatal("XTXN", "Unknown completed-transfer metadata")
          if (vif.mon_cb.pwrite && $isunknown(vif.mon_cb.pwdata))
            `uvm_fatal("XDATA", "Unknown write data in this test contract")
          t = apb_item::type_id::create("observed");
          t.addr = vif.mon_cb.paddr; t.write = vif.mon_cb.pwrite;
          t.strb = vif.mon_cb.pstrb; t.wdata = vif.mon_cb.pwdata;
          t.rdata = vif.mon_cb.prdata;
          t.status = vif.mon_cb.pslverr ? APB_ERR : APB_OK;
          ap.write(t);
        end
      end
    end
  endtask
endclass
```

**Analysis port rules.** Every subscriber receives the same handle, and `write()` is a function, so it cannot wait. Never modify a published object. A subscriber that keeps the object for later should copy it.

**AHB monitor adaptation (Q177).** Keep a one-deep "pending address phase" register. On each rising edge where HREADY is high, first complete the pending transfer using HRDATA or HWDATA and HRESP, then capture a new address phase if HTRANS is NONSEQ or SEQ. The order matters because one HREADY edge ends the previous data phase and accepts the next address.

```text
on each rising edge:
    if pending.valid and HREADY:
        publish {pending.addr, pending.write, HWDATA or HRDATA, HRESP}
        pending.valid = 0
    if HREADY and HSEL and HTRANS in {NONSEQ, SEQ}:
        pending = {HADDR, HWRITE, HSIZE, HBURST}, valid = 1
```

**Interrupt monitor and testbench (Q175).** First ask whether the interrupt is level or edge, and how it clears (often write-1-to-clear). The monitor timestamps each assertion and publishes an event. The checker predicts which source should fire, then checks the latency bound, that the line stays high until cleared, and that no interrupt fires while masked or without a cause. The test sequence services the interrupt by reading the status register and clearing the bit.

