Use this to wire components, apply configuration and overrides, and end the test for a defensible reason. The invariant is: configure and override before creation, connect after creation, and drop the final objection only after the checks have finished.

**Interview answer.** "`build_phase` runs top-down, so a parent sets config and factory overrides before it creates children. `connect_phase` runs bottom-up after every component exists. `run_phase` is the only common phase that takes time. The test raises an objection, starts the sequence, waits until the scoreboard has drained, and then drops the objection. A watchdog catches hangs."

**Construction rules.**

- A parent sets `uvm_config_db` values for children before they read them in `build_phase`.

- Install factory overrides before the `create()` call they affect. If the base class's `super.build_phase()` creates children, the override must come before that call (Q255).

- Connect sequencer to driver and monitor to scoreboard in `connect_phase`. Fail loudly when a config lookup returns nothing.

- Keep "stimulus done", "bus done", and "scoreboard drained" as three separate conditions.

```systemverilog
class apb_env extends uvm_env;
  `uvm_component_utils(apb_env)
  uvm_sequencer #(apb_item) sqr;
  apb_driver drv;
  apb_monitor mon;
  apb_mem_scoreboard scb;
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
  endfunction
  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);
    sqr = uvm_sequencer#(apb_item)::type_id::create("sqr", this);
    drv = apb_driver::type_id::create("drv", this);
    mon = apb_monitor::type_id::create("mon", this);
    scb = apb_mem_scoreboard::type_id::create("scb", this);
  endfunction
  function void connect_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.connect_phase(phase);
    drv.seq_item_port.connect(sqr.seq_item_export);
    mon.ap.connect(scb.imp);
  endfunction
endclass

class apb_test extends uvm_test;
  `uvm_component_utils(apb_test)
  apb_env env;
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
  endfunction
  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);
    env = apb_env::type_id::create("env", this);
  endfunction
  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    apb_smoke_seq s;
    phase.raise_objection(this);
    s = apb_smoke_seq::type_id::create("s");
    fork
      begin
        fork
          begin
            s.start(env.sqr);
            wait (env.scb.observed >= 16);
            if (env.scb.observed != 16 ||
                env.scb.reads_checked != 8 || env.scb.errors != 0)
              `uvm_error("COUNTS", "Unexpected observed completion counts")
          end
          begin
            #100us;
            `uvm_fatal("WATCHDOG", "Smoke test failed to complete")
          end
        join_any
        disable fork;
      end
    join
    phase.drop_objection(this);
  endtask
endclass
```

The environment owns the sequencer and driver directly to keep the example short. A reusable agent always builds its monitor but builds the driver and sequencer only when it is active. The package needs a `timeunit` for the `#100us` literal.

The top module instantiates `apb_if` as `bus`, connects the DUT, generates clock and reset, and then:

```systemverilog
initial begin
  uvm_config_db#(virtual apb_if)::set(
    null, "uvm_test_top.env.*", "vif", bus);
  run_test("apb_test");
end
```

**config_db arguments (Q225).** `set(context, instance_path, field_name, value)`. The context plus the path select which components see the value. `get(this, "", "vif", vif)` looks up the value for the calling component.

**Entry point (Q254).** `run_test()` creates the test named by `+UVM_TESTNAME` or the argument, then runs the phases. "Test class not found" (Q238) almost always means the class is missing `` `uvm_component_utils ``, so the factory cannot create it by name.

**Objections (Q221).** `run_phase` ends when every objection is dropped. A phase with no raised objection ends at time zero. A fixed drain time does not prove completion and cannot catch an objection that never drops. Use a watchdog or `set_timeout`.

**Why UVM, and its cost (Q205).** UVM gives a standard structure, factory overrides, configuration, sequences, and a register layer, so testbenches are reusable and familiar to new engineers. The cost is a steep learning curve, verbose code, slower simulation, and more setup than a small block needs.

**Simulator invocations (Q227).** Compile and elaborate once, then run one simulation per test and seed. A smoke script with N tests needs one build plus N runs. Rebuilding for every test wastes time.

**Validation list for this example.** Compile everything. Run zero-wait smoke. Inject two wait states. Inject reset in setup and in access with a reset-aware sequence. Corrupt one read byte. Exercise partial strobes. Hold PREADY low forever. Stop the clock. Legal cases must pass, reset must abort cleanly, corrupted data must mismatch, and hangs must hit the watchdog. A passing response count alone is not a passing test, so count every UVM error and assertion failure.

